adjudicate.user:
ARTICLE:
Title: {{title}}
Text excerpt: {{body}}
Publisher domain: {{publisher_domain}}
Outbound link domains: {{outlink_domains}}

CANDIDATES (profile cards):
{{candidates}}

For each candidate decide whether it is genuinely discussed AS THE SUBJECT of this article — the article is about that company and something happening to it.
- A name merely appearing somewhere in the text (ticker lists, 'other companies include X', competitor mentions, market roundups) is NOT a subject match: confidence <= 0.3.
- Subject match: the company's name (or unambiguous pronoun/reference to it) anchors the headline or the first sentence, and the reported event happens to it -> confidence >= 0.7.
- Beware namesake companies in different industries/countries; use domain overlap between article links and candidate website, geography, industry, ticker coherence as supporting evidence only.

JSON: {"matches": [{"candidate_index": number, "role": "primary"|"secondary", "confidence": number, "evidence": string}]}

batch_audit.user:
Audit every ITEM below. Look across the pile: syndicated copies of the same event should share the same keep/tag decision; roundups and commentary should be dropped even if a company name appears.

KEEP when the item reports a discrete company-subject event (funding, M&A, launch, leadership, partnership, legal/regulatory, layoffs, distress, earnings, expansion, IPO filing/plans, crowdfunding close).
DROP when it is not that — passing mentions, stock-pick lists, evergreen explainers, entertainment, sports, job ads.

NEVER drop solely because:
- the item was seen before or looks like a re-pipeline / duplicate syndication — if the headline reports a real funding/M&A/IPO/launch event TO a named company, KEEP it;
- the story mentions Form D, crowdfunding, or a stock ticker alongside a real company event — those are still company news when the headline subject is the company;
- you are trying to reduce backlog or incomplete counts — quality beats speed.

EVENT_TYPES = {{event_types}}
SECTORS = {{sectors}}

ITEMS:
{{items}}

Return JSON: {"items":[{"index":number,"keep":boolean,"reason":string,"primary_tag":string|null,"secondary_tags":string[],"sentiment":"positive"|"negative"|"neutral","sentiment_score":number,"newsworthiness":"high"|"medium"|"low","industry_primary":string|null,"industry_secondary":string[],"countries":string[],"subject_name":string|null}]}
Rules: include every ITEM index exactly once; primary_tag is an EVENT_TYPES id or null; industry_primary is a SECTORS id or null (never other_diversified); subject_name is the company the event happens TO, or null; countries are ISO-3166 alpha-2 or [].

classify_enrich.user:
Enrich this article.

TITLE: {{title}}
PUBLISHER: {{publisher}}
TEXT:
{{body}}

EVENT_TYPES = {{event_types}}
SECTORS = {{sectors}}

Rules:
- primary_tag: exactly one EVENT_TYPES id best describing THE main event, or null when the article does not report a discrete company event (market commentary, personal-finance advice, opinion, general explainers, sports/entertainment). Funding tags ONLY when capital was actually raised/invested in a company — the word 'fund' alone (mutual funds, funds focus, fund houses) is NOT a funding event, and analyst price-target changes ('PT raised to $X') are market commentary, not funding events. Product tags only for launches/releases BY the subject company.
- SETTLEMENT PRECEDENCE: when a company pays or agrees to a settlement (especially with an amount), tag legal.settlement even if joint-venture/partnership structures around it are discussed.
- secondary_tags: 0-3 additional ids; [] if none.
- sentiment about the primary company's outlook: positive|negative|neutral + score in [-1,1]; neutral 0.0 when primary_tag is null or no clear outlook.
- newsworthiness: high|medium|low materiality for a VC deal-flow platform; low when primary_tag is null.
- industry_primary / industry_secondary from SECTORS ids.
- countries: ISO-3166 alpha-2 codes of countries referenced or affected (max 4). Use [] if none.
- TOPICAL EXCLUSION: celebrity/entertainment coverage, personal-finance explainers (SIP/mutual-fund/EPF/NRI guides), sports results, horoscopes and pure politics/opinion are NOT company events: set primary_tag null, secondary_tags [], sentiment "neutral", sentiment_score 0, newsworthiness "low".

JSON schema: {"primary_tag":string|null,"secondary_tags":string[],"sentiment":"positive"|"negative"|"neutral","sentiment_score":number,"newsworthiness":"high"|"medium"|"low","industry_primary":string|null,"industry_secondary":string[],"countries":string[]}

An optional ENTITY CONTEXT block may follow the text: it carries the resolved primary company's known industries/country/type as PRIOR evidence — use it to fill industry_primary / countries when the article text alone is ambiguous; never let it override explicit contrary statements in the text.

